---
import InfoLayout from "@/layouts/InfoLayout.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<InfoLayout
  heading="Privacy"
  description="How Quick Pantry handles account information, recipes, imports, and AI client connections."
>
  <p>
    Quick Pantry is maintained by James Quick and hosted on Cloudflare. Your
    recipes, ingredients, orders, and meal plans are private to your account.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="account-data">
    <h2 id="account-data">Account and browser sessions</h2>
    <p>
      Better Auth manages email/password and Google sign-in. Account information
      includes your name, email address, and sign-in records. Passwords used for
      email/password sign-in are stored as hashes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Browser cookies and session records support sign-in and app use. Browser
      storage also remembers your theme preference. Account records and app data
      are stored using Cloudflare services.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="recipe-processing">
    <h2 id="recipe-processing">Recipe imports and search</h2>
    <p>
      When you import a recipe URL, Quick Pantry fetches content from that recipe
      site. When photo import is available, the photo you provide is sent to
      Cloudflare Workers AI to extract recipe information.
    </p>
    <p>
      Saved recipes are uploaded to Cloudflare AI Search for semantic indexing.
      Search filters results to your account. Semantic search availability can
      vary; title matching is the fallback.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ai-connections">
    <h2 id="ai-connections">Connections to AI clients</h2>
    <p>
      An MCP connection lets your chosen AI client request data and actions for
      your account. With your OAuth grant, supported tools can search and read
      recipes, create or edit recipes, import a recipe URL, and replace a weekly
      meal plan. Tool results pass to the connected AI client, whose own privacy
      terms apply to the data it receives.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can revoke OAuth connections and MCP keys from <a href="/profile">Profile</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="diagnostics">
    <h2 id="diagnostics">Diagnostic information</h2>
    <p>
      The application records diagnostic logs for errors and AI requests. These
      can include error details, request outcomes, duration, and token usage.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="privacy-requests">
    <h2 id="privacy-requests">Privacy and data requests</h2>
    <p>
      Use <a href="/support">Support</a> for questions about your data or this
      policy. Keep passwords, tokens, and private account content out of public
      issue reports.
    </p>
  </section>
</InfoLayout>
